1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,620
The police can search your car without a

2
00:00:01,620 --> 00:00:01,880
warrant.

3
00:00:02,580 --> 00:00:04,600
They've been able to for a hundred years —

4
00:00:04,600 --> 00:00:08,280
with one exception.

5
00:00:08,280 --> 00:00:09,680
You get pulled over.

6
00:00:11,280 --> 00:00:13,480
The officer walks up, glances through the

7
00:00:13,480 --> 00:00:20,000
glass, and says he's going to search the

8
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:20,280
car.

9
00:00:20,280 --> 00:00:20,980
Can he?

10
00:00:20,980 --> 00:00:21,200
When?

11
00:00:21,200 --> 00:00:24,180
And where, exactly, does that power run out?

12
00:00:24,180 --> 00:00:26,300
The answer stretches from a Prohibition

13
00:00:26,300 --> 00:00:29,500
bootlegger on a Michigan highway to a

14
00:00:29,500 --> 00:00:31,840
motorcycle hidden under a tarp in a Virginia

15
00:00:31,840 --> 00:00:32,000
driveway —

16
00:00:32,000 --> 00:00:34,900
and it quietly decides how much privacy you

17
00:00:34,900 --> 00:00:39,420
actually keep the moment you turn the key.

18
00:00:39,420 --> 00:00:43,900
Because there is one place they still can't

19
00:00:43,900 --> 00:00:44,080
go —

20
00:00:44,080 --> 00:00:48,600
and in 2018 the Supreme Court drew that

21
00:00:48,600 --> 00:00:52,380
line at the very edge of your home.

22
00:00:52,880 --> 00:00:56,680
Start with the thing that surprises almost

23
00:00:56,680 --> 00:00:56,900
everyone.

24
00:00:56,900 --> 00:01:00,640
To search your house, the police usually

25
00:01:00,640 --> 00:01:01,780
need a warrant —

26
00:01:01,780 --> 00:01:04,520
a judge's signature, probable cause written

27
00:01:05,440 --> 00:01:08,920
down, a name for the place and the

28
00:01:08,920 --> 00:01:10,120
thing they're after.

29
00:01:10,120 --> 00:01:11,960
That is the Fourth Amendment at full

30
00:01:12,760 --> 00:01:12,920
strength.

31
00:01:12,920 --> 00:01:14,440
Your car does not get that.

32
00:01:14,440 --> 00:01:16,180
And it hasn't since 1925 —

33
00:01:16,180 --> 00:01:17,320
a hundred years ago.

34
00:01:17,320 --> 00:01:18,380
It was Prohibition.

35
00:01:18,380 --> 00:01:21,620
Alcohol was illegal, and a whole underground

36
00:01:22,000 --> 00:01:24,000
economy ran it across state lines by car.

37
00:01:24,000 --> 00:01:26,740
A man named George Carroll was one of

38
00:01:26,740 --> 00:01:28,680
those runners, moving liquor along the

39
00:01:28,680 --> 00:01:30,920
highway between Detroit and Grand Rapids.

40
00:01:30,920 --> 00:01:32,840
Federal agents had been watching him.

41
00:01:32,840 --> 00:01:36,680
They stopped his car, and they didn't find

42
00:01:36,680 --> 00:01:37,840
anything in plain sight —

43
00:01:37,840 --> 00:01:40,320
so they started tearing into it.

44
00:01:40,320 --> 00:01:42,300
They ripped open the seat cushions.

45
00:01:42,300 --> 00:01:43,440
And hidden behind the upholstery of the

46
00:01:43,440 --> 00:01:45,280
seats, they pulled out sixty-eight bottles

47
00:01:45,280 --> 00:01:46,320
of whisky and gin.

48
00:01:46,320 --> 00:01:48,700
Carroll's lawyers had what sounds like a

49
00:01:48,700 --> 00:01:50,160
winning argument: no warrant, no search.

50
00:01:50,160 --> 00:01:53,060
The agents had torn his car apart on

51
00:01:55,100 --> 00:01:59,400
the side of the road with nothing on

52
00:01:59,400 --> 00:01:59,700
paper.

53
00:01:59,860 --> 00:02:03,320
The case climbed all the way to the

54
00:02:03,320 --> 00:02:03,600
Supreme Court.

55
00:02:03,600 --> 00:02:05,640
And the Court gave an answer that has

56
00:02:05,640 --> 00:02:07,280
governed your glovebox ever since.

57
00:02:07,280 --> 00:02:09,300
It drew a line —

58
00:02:09,300 --> 00:02:11,340
not around what the police wanted,

59
00:02:11,340 --> 00:02:13,380
but around the thing being searched.

60
00:02:13,380 --> 00:02:14,460
A house, the Court said,

61
00:02:14,460 --> 00:02:15,440
stays put.

62
00:02:15,440 --> 00:02:19,180
If officers have to leave and get a

63
00:02:19,180 --> 00:02:21,420
warrant, they can post someone at the door;

64
00:02:21,420 --> 00:02:23,460
the house isn't going anywhere.

65
00:02:23,460 --> 00:02:25,140
But a car is built to move.

66
00:02:25,140 --> 00:02:29,720
By the time a warrant is signed,

67
00:02:29,720 --> 00:02:30,100
a car —

68
00:02:30,100 --> 00:02:32,420
and whatever is hidden in its seats —

69
00:02:32,420 --> 00:02:34,960
can be in another county,

70
00:02:34,960 --> 00:02:35,900
another state, gone.

71
00:02:35,900 --> 00:02:38,460
Chief Justice Taft wrote that you can't

72
00:02:38,460 --> 00:02:40,400
treat the two the same,

73
00:02:41,420 --> 00:02:43,740
"because the vehicle can be quickly moved

74
00:02:43,740 --> 00:02:46,720
out of the locality or jurisdiction in which

75
00:02:48,480 --> 00:02:50,200
the warrant must be sought."

76
00:02:50,200 --> 00:02:53,660
So the Court carved out what we now

77
00:02:53,660 --> 00:02:55,380
call the automobile exception.

78
00:02:55,380 --> 00:02:59,320
If police have probable cause to believe

79
00:02:59,320 --> 00:03:01,260
your car contains contraband, they can

80
00:03:01,260 --> 00:03:01,620
search it —

81
00:03:01,620 --> 00:03:03,060
right there, no warrant.

82
00:03:03,060 --> 00:03:07,620
And this isn't a dusty relic.

83
00:03:07,620 --> 00:03:10,720
That 1925 rule is still the rule behind

84
00:03:10,720 --> 00:03:12,180
why an ordinary traffic stop can,

85
00:03:14,940 --> 00:03:16,760
under the right facts, become an open trunk

86
00:03:17,160 --> 00:03:19,140
on the shoulder of the highway.

87
00:03:19,140 --> 00:03:21,060
Not everyone agreed at the time.

88
00:03:21,060 --> 00:03:22,540
Justice McReynolds, joined by Justice

89
00:03:22,540 --> 00:03:22,880
Sutherland, dissented.

90
00:03:22,880 --> 00:03:24,620
But the majority held —

91
00:03:24,620 --> 00:03:27,540
and here's the phrase to hold onto,

92
00:03:27,540 --> 00:03:30,980
because the entire story turns on it.

93
00:03:30,980 --> 00:03:31,580
Probable cause.

94
00:03:31,580 --> 00:03:33,500
Not a warrant.

95
00:03:33,500 --> 00:03:34,080
Not certainty.

96
00:03:34,080 --> 00:03:37,540
But not a hunch either.

97
00:03:37,540 --> 00:03:40,000
We're going to see exactly where they bite.

98
00:03:40,360 --> 00:03:43,060
Here's the part movies and TV get wrong.

99
00:03:43,220 --> 00:03:45,460
The automobile exception is not a blank

100
00:03:45,460 --> 00:03:45,720
check.

101
00:03:45,720 --> 00:03:47,700
Being pulled over is not,

102
00:03:47,700 --> 00:03:49,240
by itself, permission to search your car.

103
00:03:49,680 --> 00:03:53,060
And a hunch is not enough.

104
00:03:53,060 --> 00:03:55,020
Probable cause means a fair,

105
00:03:55,020 --> 00:03:56,900
fact-based probability that there's

106
00:03:56,900 --> 00:03:58,400
contraband or evidence inside —

107
00:03:59,000 --> 00:04:00,720
something an officer can point to and

108
00:04:00,720 --> 00:04:02,820
articulate, not just a feeling.

109
00:04:02,820 --> 00:04:03,940
A busted taillight does not,

110
00:04:03,940 --> 00:04:05,700
on its own, get anyone into your trunk.

111
00:04:05,700 --> 00:04:08,100
So what does clear the bar?

112
00:04:08,580 --> 00:04:10,100
The smell of something illegal in some

113
00:04:10,100 --> 00:04:10,280
places.

114
00:04:10,280 --> 00:04:12,580
Something an officer sees in plain view on

115
00:04:12,900 --> 00:04:13,320
the seat.

116
00:04:14,100 --> 00:04:14,360
An admission.

117
00:04:15,060 --> 00:04:15,940
A trained dog's alert.

118
00:04:15,940 --> 00:04:17,140
Facts that add up.

119
00:04:17,140 --> 00:04:19,280
And once that line is crossed —

120
00:04:19,820 --> 00:04:22,180
once there really is probable cause —

121
00:04:22,180 --> 00:04:25,000
the search can go much further than people

122
00:04:25,000 --> 00:04:25,220
expect.

123
00:04:25,220 --> 00:04:27,680
Because the Supreme Court has said the reach

124
00:04:27,680 --> 00:04:30,480
of the search is defined by the thing

125
00:04:30,480 --> 00:04:31,760
they're looking for.

126
00:04:32,400 --> 00:04:35,600
If the object could fit in the glovebox,

127
00:04:35,600 --> 00:04:36,780
they can open the glovebox.

128
00:04:36,780 --> 00:04:38,480
If it could be in the trunk,

129
00:04:38,480 --> 00:04:41,880
they can open the trunk.

130
00:04:41,880 --> 00:04:45,100
If it could be inside a zipped bag

131
00:04:45,100 --> 00:04:47,960
on the back seat, they can open the

132
00:04:47,960 --> 00:04:48,120
bag.

133
00:04:48,120 --> 00:04:49,380
Locked container, loose container —

134
00:04:49,380 --> 00:04:52,580
if that's a place the thing could be,

135
00:04:52,580 --> 00:04:53,140
it's fair game.

136
00:04:53,140 --> 00:04:55,440
That reach didn't come out of nowhere.

137
00:04:55,440 --> 00:04:58,140
It was settled in two Supreme Court

138
00:04:58,140 --> 00:04:58,320
decisions —

139
00:04:58,320 --> 00:04:59,640
United States v.

140
00:04:59,640 --> 00:05:01,840
Ross in 1982, and California v.

141
00:05:01,840 --> 00:05:02,980
Acevedo in 1991 —

142
00:05:03,380 --> 00:05:05,400
which held that a closed bag or a

143
00:05:05,400 --> 00:05:08,600
locked box inside the car gets no special

144
00:05:08,600 --> 00:05:09,220
shield of its own.

145
00:05:09,220 --> 00:05:11,740
If the thing they have probable cause to

146
00:05:11,740 --> 00:05:13,580
find could be in there,

147
00:05:13,580 --> 00:05:14,680
they can open it.

148
00:05:14,840 --> 00:05:16,380
But that same rule cuts the other way,

149
00:05:16,380 --> 00:05:22,340
and it's the closest thing you have to

150
00:05:24,060 --> 00:05:24,420
a leash.

151
00:05:24,420 --> 00:05:28,560
The search has to fit the reason for

152
00:05:28,560 --> 00:05:28,980
it.

153
00:05:29,660 --> 00:05:34,840
If the probable cause is about a stolen

154
00:05:34,840 --> 00:05:37,520
fifty-inch television, that does not justify

155
00:05:37,520 --> 00:05:38,340
opening a tiny pill bottle —

156
00:05:38,340 --> 00:05:40,220
a TV can't hide in there.

157
00:05:40,220 --> 00:05:41,920
The bigger the thing they're chasing,

158
00:05:42,360 --> 00:05:44,840
the fewer the places they're allowed to

159
00:05:44,840 --> 00:05:45,120
look.

160
00:05:45,120 --> 00:05:48,840
The smaller it is, the more of your

161
00:05:48,840 --> 00:05:50,040
car opens up.

162
00:05:50,040 --> 00:05:52,280
They don't even have to prove a separate

163
00:05:52,280 --> 00:05:53,800
emergency, though.

164
00:05:54,140 --> 00:05:56,700
The Court has been blunt about it: if

165
00:05:56,700 --> 00:05:59,400
the car is drivable and there's probable

166
00:05:59,400 --> 00:06:00,240
cause, that's enough —

167
00:06:00,240 --> 00:06:03,860
no extra excuse required.

168
00:06:03,860 --> 00:06:08,340
The Justices spelled that out again in the

169
00:06:08,340 --> 00:06:09,860
nineteen-nineties: a car that's readily

170
00:06:09,860 --> 00:06:11,200
mobile, plus probable cause, needs nothing

171
00:06:11,200 --> 00:06:11,420
more.

172
00:06:11,420 --> 00:06:12,600
No ticking clock.

173
00:06:12,600 --> 00:06:15,040
No proof the driver was about to speed

174
00:06:15,040 --> 00:06:15,280
off.

175
00:06:15,280 --> 00:06:18,220
The car being a car is the emergency.

176
00:06:19,080 --> 00:06:21,520
Now step back and feel what that means

177
00:06:21,900 --> 00:06:22,440
for you.

178
00:06:22,440 --> 00:06:24,340
Your car is where you keep your bag,

179
00:06:24,340 --> 00:06:26,940
your phone, your paperwork, half of your

180
00:06:26,940 --> 00:06:29,080
private life while it's in motion.

181
00:06:29,080 --> 00:06:30,980
And it carries far thinner protection than

182
00:06:30,980 --> 00:06:31,460
your living room —

183
00:06:31,460 --> 00:06:34,400
for a reason written down before your

184
00:06:34,400 --> 00:06:34,960
grandparents were born.

185
00:06:34,960 --> 00:06:37,560
Which raises the only question that's left.

186
00:06:37,560 --> 00:06:40,560
If the car is this exposed —

187
00:06:40,560 --> 00:06:43,280
is there anywhere the police still cannot

188
00:06:43,280 --> 00:06:43,600
follow it?

189
00:06:43,600 --> 00:06:46,600
To find the line, go to Virginia —

190
00:06:46,600 --> 00:06:48,420
and a motorcycle that kept getting away.

191
00:06:48,420 --> 00:06:50,880
An officer had twice been outrun by the

192
00:06:50,880 --> 00:06:53,920
same distinctive orange-and-black bike, and

193
00:06:54,940 --> 00:06:56,740
he suspected it was stolen.

194
00:06:56,740 --> 00:07:00,740
Then, one day, he tracked it down.

195
00:07:00,740 --> 00:07:01,840
Not on a street.

196
00:07:02,500 --> 00:07:03,500
To a house.

197
00:07:03,500 --> 00:07:06,300
And there it was: parked in the driveway,

198
00:07:06,300 --> 00:07:08,300
pulled up close to the side of the

199
00:07:08,300 --> 00:07:11,040
home, with a tarp thrown over it.

200
00:07:11,040 --> 00:07:17,260
So the officer walked up the driveway.

201
00:07:17,260 --> 00:07:18,500
He lifted the tarp.

202
00:07:18,500 --> 00:07:22,820
He ran the plate and the vehicle number —

203
00:07:23,460 --> 00:07:25,420
and it came back stolen.

204
00:07:25,420 --> 00:07:27,640
Then he waited for the man staying there,

205
00:07:27,640 --> 00:07:30,660
Ryan Collins, and arrested him.

206
00:07:30,660 --> 00:07:34,360
Here's why this case is the whole ballgame.

207
00:07:35,720 --> 00:07:37,340
The officer had real reason to think that

208
00:07:37,340 --> 00:07:38,200
bike was stolen —

209
00:07:38,800 --> 00:07:39,480
that's probable cause.

210
00:07:39,480 --> 00:07:43,880
And if that motorcycle had been sitting on

211
00:07:43,880 --> 00:07:48,340
a public street, lifting the tarp is a

212
00:07:48,340 --> 00:07:49,420
clean, legal, automobile-exception search.

213
00:07:49,920 --> 00:07:51,500
So Virginia argued: same thing here.

214
00:07:51,500 --> 00:07:52,820
A vehicle, probable cause, done —

215
00:07:52,820 --> 00:07:57,260
it shouldn't matter that it was in a

216
00:07:57,260 --> 00:07:57,480
driveway.

217
00:07:57,480 --> 00:08:01,300
But think about how much that position was

218
00:08:01,300 --> 00:08:03,620
really asking for.

219
00:08:03,620 --> 00:08:06,780
To search a car parked at a stranger's

220
00:08:06,780 --> 00:08:09,120
public curb, an officer needs nothing but

221
00:08:09,120 --> 00:08:09,560
probable cause.

222
00:08:10,120 --> 00:08:13,360
Virginia wanted that same power to include

223
00:08:13,360 --> 00:08:14,680
walking onto private property —

224
00:08:14,680 --> 00:08:18,360
up the driveway, past the point where a

225
00:08:18,360 --> 00:08:19,500
home begins.

226
00:08:19,500 --> 00:08:24,300
If that were allowed, the most exposed thing

227
00:08:24,300 --> 00:08:26,380
you own, your car, could become the key

228
00:08:26,480 --> 00:08:29,680
that unlocks the most protected place you

229
00:08:29,680 --> 00:08:32,060
have, your home.

230
00:08:32,580 --> 00:08:34,300
The Supreme Court said no.

231
00:08:34,300 --> 00:08:37,020
In 2018, by a vote of eight to

232
00:08:37,020 --> 00:08:39,680
one, the Court held that the car-search

233
00:08:39,680 --> 00:08:45,040
power does not reach onto the ground that

234
00:08:45,040 --> 00:08:46,540
belongs to your home.

235
00:08:46,540 --> 00:08:48,340
There's a word for that ground.

236
00:08:48,340 --> 00:08:50,200
The porch, the front steps,

237
00:08:51,160 --> 00:08:53,960
the strip of driveway pressed against the

238
00:08:53,960 --> 00:08:54,180
walls —

239
00:08:55,260 --> 00:08:58,020
the law calls it the curtilage.

240
00:08:58,020 --> 00:09:04,420
And curtilage is treated as part of the

241
00:09:04,420 --> 00:09:04,860
home.

242
00:09:04,860 --> 00:09:07,380
It gets the home's full protection.

243
00:09:07,380 --> 00:09:10,440
And that isn't a technicality.

244
00:09:10,440 --> 00:09:12,760
The home is the ground the Fourth Amendment

245
00:09:12,760 --> 00:09:13,560
guards most fiercely.

246
00:09:13,560 --> 00:09:16,660
The Court's point was simple: you cannot let

247
00:09:16,660 --> 00:09:20,700
a rule built for the open road quietly

248
00:09:20,700 --> 00:09:24,260
swallow the most protected ground a person

249
00:09:24,260 --> 00:09:24,560
owns.

250
00:09:24,560 --> 00:09:27,100
Where you park does not shrink your home.

251
00:09:27,100 --> 00:09:28,420
Your home enlarges what it protects.

252
00:09:30,900 --> 00:09:33,100
Justice Sotomayor, writing for the Court,

253
00:09:33,100 --> 00:09:38,400
said it in one line worth keeping: "The

254
00:09:38,400 --> 00:09:40,220
scope of the automobile exception extends no

255
00:09:40,220 --> 00:09:41,920
further than the automobile itself."

256
00:09:41,920 --> 00:09:43,660
So both things are true at once.

257
00:09:43,660 --> 00:09:46,420
Out on the street, that same search is

258
00:09:46,420 --> 00:09:46,840
fine.

259
00:09:46,840 --> 00:09:51,220
But the instant the car is tucked into

260
00:09:51,220 --> 00:09:53,960
the protected ground hugging your house,

261
00:09:55,860 --> 00:09:57,600
the hundred-year exception runs out of road.

262
00:09:57,600 --> 00:10:00,220
To step onto that ground and search,

263
00:10:00,220 --> 00:10:01,580
police need a warrant —

264
00:10:01,580 --> 00:10:03,660
or a completely different exception.

265
00:10:03,660 --> 00:10:04,540
Only Justice Alito dissented —

266
00:10:04,540 --> 00:10:08,860
worried the Court was letting a technicality

267
00:10:08,860 --> 00:10:11,720
shield a bike he had strong reason to

268
00:10:11,840 --> 00:10:12,520
believe was stolen.

269
00:10:12,520 --> 00:10:14,100
But he stood alone.

270
00:10:14,100 --> 00:10:15,620
The line held.

271
00:10:15,620 --> 00:10:17,480
So now you know where the line falls.

272
00:10:17,480 --> 00:10:19,800
The question that actually matters is what

273
00:10:19,800 --> 00:10:23,080
it means the next time it's your window

274
00:10:23,080 --> 00:10:25,880
the officer is walking up to.

275
00:10:25,880 --> 00:10:29,900
So here's the actual map of your rights

276
00:10:29,900 --> 00:10:31,780
on the road —

277
00:10:31,780 --> 00:10:33,600
the one almost nobody is handed.

278
00:10:33,600 --> 00:10:36,980
On a public street or a routine stop,

279
00:10:38,220 --> 00:10:41,520
your car can be searched without a warrant —

280
00:10:41,520 --> 00:10:43,580
but only with probable cause,

281
00:10:45,040 --> 00:10:47,120
and only as far as the thing they're

282
00:10:47,120 --> 00:10:50,100
looking for could actually be.

283
00:10:50,100 --> 00:10:50,800
Being arrested doesn't automatically open

284
00:10:53,780 --> 00:10:54,320
the car either.

285
00:10:54,480 --> 00:10:56,060
In a case called Gant,

286
00:10:56,060 --> 00:10:57,880
the Court said that once you're handcuffed

287
00:10:57,880 --> 00:11:00,700
and set away from the vehicle,

288
00:11:00,700 --> 00:11:02,520
officers generally can't search it just

289
00:11:02,520 --> 00:11:03,580
because they arrested you —

290
00:11:03,580 --> 00:11:06,700
not unless it's reasonable to think evidence

291
00:11:06,700 --> 00:11:08,800
of that specific offense is inside.

292
00:11:09,880 --> 00:11:12,540
The protection can even follow you into a

293
00:11:12,540 --> 00:11:12,780
rental.

294
00:11:12,780 --> 00:11:15,180
The Court has said that if you're lawfully

295
00:11:15,180 --> 00:11:16,420
driving a rental car, you can keep a

296
00:11:16,421 --> 00:11:16,921
real expectation of privacy in it —

297
00:11:16,922 --> 00:11:17,422
even if your name isn't on the paperwork.

298
00:11:17,423 --> 00:11:17,923
And there's one honest gray area worth

299
00:11:17,924 --> 00:11:18,424
naming.

300
00:11:18,425 --> 00:11:18,925
For years, in many places,

301
00:11:18,926 --> 00:11:19,426
the smell of marijuana alone was treated as

302
00:11:19,427 --> 00:11:19,927
probable cause.

303
00:11:19,928 --> 00:11:20,428
But the Supreme Court has never set a

304
00:11:20,429 --> 00:11:20,929
single nationwide rule on that —

305
00:11:20,930 --> 00:11:21,430
and in states that have legalized or

306
00:11:21,431 --> 00:11:21,931
decriminalized it, several courts now say

307
00:11:21,932 --> 00:11:22,432
the odor by itself isn't enough anymore.

308
00:11:22,433 --> 00:11:22,933
On that one, the honest answer is: it

309
00:11:22,934 --> 00:11:23,434
depends where you are.

310
00:11:23,435 --> 00:11:23,935
So what does all of this mean the

311
00:11:23,936 --> 00:11:24,436
next time you're actually pulled over?

312
00:11:24,437 --> 00:11:24,937
You don't have to be a lawyer.

313
00:11:24,938 --> 00:11:25,438
Just carry the shape of it: they need

314
00:11:25,439 --> 00:11:25,939
a real, articulable reason —

315
00:11:25,940 --> 00:11:26,440
not a mood —

316
00:11:26,441 --> 00:11:26,941
before they can search; the search has to

317
00:11:26,942 --> 00:11:27,442
match the thing they're after; and the

318
00:11:27,443 --> 00:11:27,943
ground pressed against your house is not the

319
00:11:27,944 --> 00:11:28,444
road.

320
00:11:28,445 --> 00:11:28,945
But the throughline is a century old,

321
00:11:28,946 --> 00:11:29,446
and it's simple.

322
00:11:29,447 --> 00:11:29,947
Your car trades privacy for mobility.

323
00:11:29,948 --> 00:11:30,448
The exception is broad —

324
00:11:30,449 --> 00:11:30,949
broader than most people believe —

325
00:11:30,950 --> 00:11:31,450
and it isn't a myth.

326
00:11:31,451 --> 00:11:31,951
It's just not infinite.

327
00:11:31,952 --> 00:11:32,452
It stops, exactly, at the edge of your

328
00:11:32,453 --> 00:11:32,953
home.

329
00:11:32,954 --> 00:11:33,454
If you drive, that line is yours —

330
00:11:33,455 --> 00:11:33,955
and most people go their whole lives never

331
00:11:33,956 --> 00:11:34,456
knowing exactly where it falls.

332
00:11:34,457 --> 00:11:34,957
If knowing where your rights actually stop

333
00:11:34,958 --> 00:11:35,458
changed how you'll see the next traffic

334
00:11:35,459 --> 00:11:35,959
stop, hit like —

335
00:11:35,960 --> 00:11:36,460
it honestly helps more drivers find this.

336
00:11:36,461 --> 00:11:36,961
And tell me in the comments: were you

337
00:11:36,962 --> 00:11:37,462
taught that police need a warrant to search

338
00:11:37,463 --> 00:11:37,963
your car?

339
00:11:37,964 --> 00:11:38,464
Because for a hundred years,

340
00:11:38,465 --> 00:11:38,965
they haven't.
